<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonld={jsonld} navVariant="inner">
<section class="hero">
<div class="wrap">
<span class="eyebrow">Stage 2 — During production</span>
<h1>Catch it at 30% complete. Fix it in hours, not containers.</h1>
<p class="lead">The cheapest defect is the one caught mid-run. DUPRO inspects at 20–50% completion—components, early assemblies, first finished pieces—while rework costs hours, not containers.</p>
<div class="hero-ctas"><a class="btn btn-solid" href="/#book">Book an inspection</a><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/why-independent/">Why independent</a></div>
</div>
</section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>What a during-production inspection checks</h2></div></div><div class="grid"><a class="card rv" href="/pre-shipment-inspection-india/"><h3>Moisture, before assembly hides it</h3><p>Core readings on components and panels—the point where a skipped kiln cycle is still fixable.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Dimensions &amp; first-offs</h3><p>First completed pieces measured against spec before the whole batch repeats an error.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/#book"><h3>Finish direction</h3><p>Early finished pieces vs golden sample, before 200 more are sprayed the wrong shade.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/production-monitoring-india/"><h3>Schedule truth</h3><p>Real progress vs claimed progress—the honest answer to "will it make the vessel?"</p></a></div></div></section><section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Why 20–50% completion is the window</h2></div></div><p class="prose rv">A during-production inspection is timed, not routine. Too early and there is nothing built to measure; too late and the whole run already carries the fault you came to catch. The 20–50% mark is the point where the first components are drying, the first pieces are assembled and the finish line has sprayed enough to show its hand — while there is still raw batch left to correct. Every DUPRO finding is one with somewhere to go.</p><p class="prose rv">Three of those findings only exist at this stage. <strong>Core moisture</strong>, read on components and panels before glue and assembly seal it inside: a shortened kiln cycle feels dry on the surface and stays wet in the core, and by pre-shipment it is already built into a table that will crack in a heated home. <strong>First-off dimensions</strong>, measured against your spec before the batch repeats an error four hundred more times. And <strong>finish</strong>, checked against your golden sample before the next two hundred pieces are sprayed the wrong shade. A pre-shipment inspection can only report these; a during-production inspection can still change them.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>Three orders where a DUPRO earns its fee</h2></div></div><div class="grid"><a class="card rv" href="/first-order-shield/"><h3>A first order with a new factory</h3><p>You do not yet know how this workshop dries wood, holds a dimension or matches a finish. A during-production inspection reads all three at 30% complete — which is why it is paired with a pre-shipment inspection in the First Order Shield.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/monsoon-watch/"><h3>Anything in production, July to September</h3><p>Monsoon humidity re-wets seasoned stock and stalls curing. A mid-run core-moisture reading catches wood that will move in a dry destination home before the whole batch is built on it.</p></a><a class="card rv" href="/production-monitoring-india/"><h3>Repeat runs and large volumes</h3><p>The first finished pieces are the tell. Caught against spec and the golden sample before the run repeats, a first-off check turns a 500-piece rework into a five-piece correction.</p></a></div></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap prose-wrap"><div class="sec-head"><div><h2>How a during-production inspection runs</h2></div></div><p class="prose rv">Booking takes a service, a cluster, a date and your product spec; we confirm within two hours and agree the checklist, AQL and golden-sample references before the visit. On site, the inspector checks early finished pieces against your AQL plan and golden sample, takes multiple pin-meter moisture readings at the <em>core</em> of components rather than the surface, and measures first-offs against your drawing. Within 24 hours you receive a photographed report: every finding measured, referenced to the standard or your spec and given a severity class, ending in a plain recommendation — carry on, correct now, or hold the run. Same-day availability in Jodhpur and Jaipur; the fixed fee, from $249, is published on booking and paid by you, never by the factory.</p></div></section>
<section id="faq">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><div><h2>During-production inspection FAQ</h2></div></div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details class="rv"><summary>When should a during-production inspection be booked?</summary><p>At 20–50% completion — early enough that findings still change the run, late enough that there is real product to inspect.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>What does DUPRO check that a pre-shipment inspection cannot?</summary><p>Core moisture readings on components and panels before assembly hides them, first-off dimensions before the batch repeats an error, and early finished pieces against the golden sample before the rest are sprayed.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>How much does a during-production inspection cost?</summary><p>From $249, with the fixed fee published on booking. The report follows within 24 hours of the visit.</p></details>
      <details class="rv"><summary>Should DUPRO be combined with a pre-shipment inspection?</summary><p>On first orders and monsoon-season orders we recommend DUPRO plus PSI as a pair. That combination is included in the <a href="/first-order-shield/">First Order Shield</a> bundle.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
